-- title:
--   Theorem 2.9 — every finite POMDP has $\varepsilon$-optimal finite-memory strategies for the liminf-average payoff
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma=(\mathcal K,\mathcal A,\mathcal S,q,g)$ be a POMDP with finite state space, finite action set and finite signal set, an arbitrary transition function $q:\mathcal K\times\mathcal A\to\Delta(\mathcal K\times\mathcal S)$ and a reward function $g$ with values in $[0,1]$. For every initial belief $p_1\in\Delta(\mathcal K)$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a strategy $\sigma$ that has finite memory (it is generated by a finite-state transducer) and is $\varepsilon$-optimal:
--
--   $$\gamma^{p_1}_\infty(\sigma)\ge v_\infty(p_1)-\varepsilon,$$
--
--   where $\gamma^{p_1}_\infty(\sigma)=\mathbb E^{p_1}_\sigma\big(\liminf_{n}\frac1n\sum_{m=1}^nG_m\big)$ and $v_\infty(p_1)$ is the supremum of $\gamma^{p_1}_\infty$ over all pure strategies.
--
--   No bound on the memory size in terms of the data of $\Gamma$ is claimed; by Remark 2.10 none exists.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: ∆(K), ∆(A) probability vectors; q: K × A → ∆(K × S); histories in ∪_m (A × S)^{m−1}; plays in (K × A × S)^ℕ; K, A, S finite → stdSimplex ℝ K / stdSimplex ℝ A; q as PMF (K × S); histories as chronological List (A × S); plays as ℕ → PlayStep A S K with coordinate n+1 = (A_{n+1}, S_{n+1}, K_{n+2}); K, A, S finite and nonempty; reason: Lean representations of the same objects; nonemptiness is implicit in the paper (p1 ∈ ∆(K), σ needs an action, q(k,a) ∈ ∆(K × S) needs a signal); the play space is a bijective re-indexing
--   - encoding: σ = (σu, σa, M, m0) with M a finite set → ∃ M : Type with Finite M, m0, σa, σu generating σ by a left fold over the history; reason: the standard transducer semantics, which the paper leaves implicit
-- source:
--   Chatterjee, Saona & Ziliotto, Finite-memory strategies in POMDPs with Long-run Average Objectives, arXiv:1904.13360v2, Theorem 2.9, p.3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FiniteMemPOMDP_IsEpsOptimal
import Definitions.Def_FiniteMemPOMDP_IsFiniteMemory

namespace FiniteMemPOMDP

/-- **Theorem 2.9 — ε-optimal finite-memory strategies exist.**
Chatterjee, Saona & Ziliotto, arXiv:1904.13360v2, Theorem 2.9, p.3: "For every POMDP Γ, initial
belief p1 and ε > 0, there exists an ε-optimal finite-memory strategy in Γ(p1)."

For all finite nonempty sets of states `K`, actions `A` and signals `S`, every POMDP
`Γ = (K, A, S, q, g)` (any joint transition `q : K × A → ∆(K × S)`, any reward `g` with values in
`[0, 1]`), every initial belief `p1 ∈ ∆(K)` and every `ε > 0`, there is a pure strategy `σ` generated
by a finite-state transducer with `γ^{p1}_∞(σ) ≥ v∞(p1) − ε`, where `γ^{p1}_∞` is the expected
liminf of the average rewards and `v∞(p1)` the supremum of `γ^{p1}_∞` over all pure strategies.

**Formalization Note** `K`, `A`, `S` are nonempty (implicit in the paper: `∆(K)` contains `p1`,
a strategy needs an action, `q(k, a) ∈ ∆(K × S)` needs a signal). -/
theorem theorem_2_9 {K A S : Type} [Fintype K] [DecidableEq K] [Nonempty K] [Fintype A] [DecidableEq A]
    [Nonempty A] [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Nonempty S]
    (Γ : POMDP K A S) (p1 : stdSimplex ℝ K) (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) :
    ∃ σ : List (A × S) → A, IsFiniteMemory σ ∧ IsEpsOptimal Γ p1 ε σ := by sorry

end FiniteMemPOMDP
